Print a tree of documentation paths

Builds a directory-like tree from all page paths stored in the documentation
database.

Examples:
  snouty docs tree
  snouty docs tree --depth 2
  snouty docs tree sdk

Each line shows one path segment. A line whose path is a page also shows the
page's title, after " - ". For example, `snouty docs tree rust` prints:

  reference
  ┗━━ sdk - SDKs
      ┗━━ rust - Rust SDK
          ┣━━ example - Rust SDK tutorial, times10 function
          ┣━━ instrumentation - Rust instrumentation
          ┗━━ legacy_instrumentation - Legacy Rust instrumentation

To get the path of a page, join the segments of its parent lines and its own
line, from the top level down, with "/", for example reference/sdk/rust/example.
`snouty docs show` accepts this path.

With --json, the tree prints as one JSON object, {"children": [node, ...]}. Each
node has name, the last segment of its path, and path. A node has title when it
is a page, and then `snouty docs show` accepts its path. A node has children,
sorted by name, when it has child nodes that --depth did not remove. When no
page matches, the output is {"children": []}.

Usage: snouty docs tree [OPTIONS] [FILTER]

Arguments:
  [FILTER]
          Optional case-insensitive filter applied to page paths and titles

Options:
  -d, --depth <DEPTH>
          Limit output to nodes at this depth or shallower

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

      --offline
          Don't check for documentation updates

      --json
          Print one JSON object

      --verbose
          Log API requests to stderr (authentication tokens redacted)

      --settings <SETTINGS>
          Path to the snouty settings file (default: ./.snouty.toml; overrides
          SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH)

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Settings profile to select (overrides ANTITHESIS_PROFILE)
